2474. Customers With Strictly Increasing Purchases
Table: Orders
Column Name	Type
order_id	int
customer_id	int
order_date	date
price	int

order_id is the primary key of this table.

Each row represents an order made by a customer on a given date with a given price.

Each customer can have multiple orders.

order_date values are unique per customer (i.e., a customer cannot place two orders on the same date).

Definition

A customer has strictly increasing purchases if:

For every consecutive pair of orders (ordered by order_date) made by the customer,
the price of the later order is strictly greater than the price of the earlier order.

Customers with only one order are considered to have strictly increasing purchases.

Task

Write an SQL query to find the customer_id(s) of customers whose purchases are strictly increasing over time.

Return the result table in any order.

Example
Input

Orders

order_id	customer_id	order_date	price
1	1	2021-01-01	10
2	1	2021-01-02	20
3	1	2021-01-03	30
4	2	2021-01-01	20
5	2	2021-01-02	20
6	3	2021-01-01	50
Output
customer_id
1
3

Solution 1
==========
WITH prevs AS (
  SELECT
    customer_id,
    price,
    LAG(price) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY order_date) AS prev_price
  FROM Orders
)
SELECT
  customer_id
FROM prevs
GROUP BY customer_id
HAVING MAX(CASE WHEN prev_price IS NOT NULL AND prev_price >= price THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) = 0;


Solution 2
===========
SELECT DISTINCT customer_id
FROM Orders
WHERE customer_id NOT IN (
  SELECT o1.customer_id
  FROM Orders o1
  JOIN Orders o2
    ON o1.customer_id = o2.customer_id
   AND o1.order_date < o2.order_date
   AND o1.price >= o2.price
);
